Flow of the Code (High-level Steps)
=======================================

1)Padding the Packet:
-------------------------

	The packet (skb) is padded to meet the minimum size requirements. If padding fails, the packet is dropped.
	minimum size of theethernet frame is 64B and if the frame is smaller than it then it is padded.
	
	It is done using the fucntion:  *** skb_padto(skb, tx_packet_min) ***

	Padding is necessary : 
	----------------------
			
		-Hardware (NIC) are sesigned to handle packets of a certain size and is often done for performance reasons, as handling small packets
		 requires more overhead relative to their data size.
		
		-Smaller packets are more prone to errors, and having a minimum size helps ensure that error detection mechanism (like CRC checks)
						
>>The function skb_padto(skb, tx_packet_min) ensures that the size of the packet meets the minimum transmission size (tx_packet_min). If the packet is smaller than this threshold, padding is added.		

2) Length Determination:
---------------------------

	The length of the packet (len) is adjusted to ensure it is at least the minimum size(tx_packet_min).
	
		*** len = skb->len < tx_packet_min ? tx_packet_min : skb->len; ***


3) Timestamp Handling:
-----------------------
	Timestamping is a local mechanism used for performance measurement, debugging, and synchronization on a single device. It is not used for
	inter-device communication and doesn't travel with the packet across the network.
	
	+-----------------------+   <- Packet Data (Ethernet Header, IP Header, Transport Headers, Payload)
	| Ethernet Header       |	<- This is the part that is sent over the network. 
	| IP Header             |   
	| TCP/UDP Header        |
	| Payload               |
	+-----------------------+   <- This is the part that is device-specific and not sent over network.
	| Metadata:             |   <- Metadata area (auxiliary data, flags, etc.)
	| - tstamp (timestamp)  |   <- The timestamp (for TX or RX) is stored here
	| - tx_flags            |   <- Various flags like SKBTX_HW_TSTAMP, SKBTX_IN_PROGRESS
	| - skb references      |   <- Pointers to buffers, etc.
	+-----------------------+	


	If hardware timestamping is enabled and possible, the packet is marked to indicate that timestamping is in progress by NIC.
	
	
	TX Timestamping (Transmit):
	----------------------------

		>When the packet is ready to be transmitted, and hardware timestamping is enabled, the driver checks if the packet should be
		 timestamped (using the flag SKBTX_HW_TSTAMP).

		>If timestamping is enabled, the NIC will add the timestamp at the point of transmission, and this timestamp will be stored in the 
		skb->tstamp field.
		
