5) DMA Channel Selection:
----------------------------

	The correct DMA channel for the selected queue is fetched.
	
		txch = cpsw->txv[q_idx].ch;
		txq = netdev_get_tx_queue(ndev, q_idx);


6) Submitting the Packet to the DMA Channel:
------------------------------------------------

	The packet is submitted to the DMA engine for transmission through the selected channel using the fucntion : 
	
	``` ret = cpdma_chan_submit(txch, skb, skb->data, skb->len, priv->emac_port + cpsw->data.dual_emac); ```
	
		What happens here?: The packet is submitted to the selected DMA channel (txch) for transmission. The function provides:

		skb: The packet buffer.
		skb->data: The actual data to be transmitted.
		skb->len: The length of the data to be transmitted.
		priv->emac_port + cpsw->data.dual_emac: The port identifier for the EMAC (Ethernet MAC) in dual-emac mode.


7) Handling Transmission Errors:
-----------------------------------
	If the DMA submission fails, the packet is dropped, and the transmit queue is stopped.
	
	/* If there is no more tx desc left free then we need to
	 * tell the kernel to stop sending us tx frames.
	 */
	 
		``` if (unlikely(ret != 0)) {
				cpsw_err(priv, tx_err, "desc submit failed\n");
				goto fail;
			}  		
			
			unlikely macro coz its very rare to have no free descriptors, There are 1024 to 4096 descriptors per queue.		```

	


8) Checking for Free TX Descriptors :
---------------------------------------

	The system checks if there are free descriptors available in the DMA channel. If there are no free descriptors, the kernel is instructed to
	 stop sending packets on the queue.
	 
   ```if (unlikely(!cpdma_check_free_tx_desc(txch))) {
    	netif_tx_stop_queue(txq);

    	/* Barrier, so that stop_queue visible to other cpus */
    	
    	smp_mb__after_atomic();

			if (cpdma_check_free_tx_desc(txch))   //checking if there is freee desc , then lift the barrier
				netif_tx_wake_queue(txq);
}											````

	>What happens here?: This checks if there are free transmission descriptors available in the DMA channel. If there are no free
	 descriptors, the transmit queue is stopped using netif_tx_stop_queue(txq).

	>Why the barrier?: smp_mb__after_atomic() ensures that the stop operation is visible to other CPUs before proceeding.

	>Resuming transmission: If descriptors become available again, netif_tx_wake_queue(txq) is called to resume transmission.


9) Return Status:
-------------------

	The function returns either NETDEV_TX_OK (success) or NETDEV_TX_BUSY (failure), depending on whether the packet was transmitted successfully or not to teh hardware.
	
	
